// Task T12 · Create a customer (Sales hands the form) + Task T24 · Sales voucher entry — the database · rev 15 (20-Nov-2026)
//
// REV 15 (20-Nov, Vijay: "the actual cost of goods comes from inventory"): the sale also posts its COST, in the SAME voucher and
//   the same journal entry (one entry per invoice: his journal_entry_one_forward_entry_per_document). The invoice gives the item
//   and quantity; inventory gives the cost; the company's method decides which cost: FIFO (the oldest lot first, each at its own
//   cost) or weighted average (value ÷ quantity). LIFO is not allowed (Ind AS 2 / AS 2). Entry (rule R13, in code):
//   Dr 501040 Cost of Goods Sold (proposed: the CA's key says "COGS"; his chart has no such ledger, G18) · Cr 106020 Finished Goods,
//   one line per lot (FIFO) or per item (weighted average). +1 table, inv.stock_lot (a ROUGH STUB of inventory, not accounting:
//   the inventory module owns it, T8 / S4, G3); +3 columns on fin.sales_invoice_line (inventory_item_code: his purchase line has it
//   too, so that twin difference is gone; quantity; stock_lot_id); +1 on fin.sales_invoice (costing_method). The journal: unchanged.
//   This replaces rev 4–14's "the cost is posted by Stores' delivery challan (G30)": the CA's specimens are invoice-only.
//
// REV 14 (18-Nov, Vijay: "follow Manoj's model"): T24 is the TWIN of Manoj's purchase voucher, as he built it. His purchase side
//   adds 3 tables (fin.purchase_invoice, _line, _history) and posts into the 2 shared journal tables (fin.journal_entry,
//   fin.journal_line). Ours: fin.sales_invoice (26), fin.sales_invoice_line (8), fin.sales_invoice_history (10), into the same
//   journal. Rev 13's fin.voucher, voucher_gst, voucher_line, voucher_history and posting_rule are REMOVED: no GST columns (GST =
//   lines on the output GST ledgers), no rule table (the rules run in code, as his). G38 closed: we follow his design.
//   NOTE THE NAME: fin.sales_invoice here is the VOUCHER (26 columns, as his purchase_invoice is his), not the 62-column
//   invoice of revs 5–11 (Billing's, removed in rev 12). No item, HSN, IRN or e-way bill column comes back.
//
// REV 13 (18-Nov, Vijay): ONE voucher design for every voucher type. T24's fin.sales_voucher / _line / _history become
//   fin.voucher / fin.voucher_line / fin.voucher_history with voucher_type = 'sales'; the invoice's GST amounts move to
//   fin.voucher_gst (shared by sales, purchase, credit and debit notes). Added: source_event_id (never booked twice) and
//   due_date (what a receivable needs for ageing). Receipt, payment, journal … will add no table. To agree with Manoj: G38.
//
// REV 12 (18-Nov, Vijay): the Accounts Executive does NOT raise the sales invoice. Billing raises it (number, IRN, lines, GST)
//   and hands it over; the AE books its ACCOUNTING ENTRY: ledgers suggested by the posting rule, the AE may pick others; Dr = Cr;
//   the GST lines = the invoice's GST; Submit → the Accounts Manager approves → the entry posts. So T24 keeps only the voucher:
//   fin.sales_voucher (25 columns, all on the AE's screen), fin.sales_voucher_line (the entry), fin.sales_voucher_history,
//   fin.posting_rule. REMOVED (they are Billing's): fin.sales_invoice, fin.sales_invoice_line, fin.e_invoice, fin.e_way_bill,
//   fin.document_series, fin.export_lut; fin.sales_invoice_entry_line / _history become fin.sales_voucher_line / _history.
//   Also no longer needed: the 2 keys T24 had added on fin.debtor_location (the invoice's bill-to / ship-to links).
//   The rev 5–11 notes below describe the earlier invoice design; they are kept as history.
//
// HOW TO READ THE DIAGRAM (dbdiagram.io): four coloured groups.
//   1 P&C · Business Core (teal): WHO the party is. Another team's tables; we read them, never change them.
//   2 T12 · Create a customer (indigo): the customer's account with us, and the request that opened it.
//   3 Books core · Manoj, built (amber): branches, years, numbering, the journal. Shared by purchase and sales.
//   4 T24 Sales voucher (green): the twin of Manoj's purchase voucher. The voucher, its lines, its history.
//
// REV 10 (18-Nov, Vijay: "the sales invoice does not contain this many fields"): fin.sales_invoice now holds what the screen
//   shows or the system must keep; 5 columns that only repeated or held a constant are removed: posting_date (= invoice_date;
//   the journal entry keeps its own), reverse_charge (always No for our sales: printed as a word), narration (written into
//   fin.journal_entry.narration), cess_amount (nil, on no CA invoice), tcs_amount (the rate waits for the CA, G18: added when
//   confirmed); and fin.sales_invoice_line.cess_amount. 62 columns remain, each in one of 5 groups (see the table's note).
//   Rev 11 (Vijay: "where do we enter all these in the UI?"): every invoice column is mapped to its place on the screen (the
//   table's note, and the walkthrough's full table). 57 of 62 are typed or shown; 5 are the system columns every table has.
//   fin.sales_invoice_line.concession_notification is removed: the screen has no field for it (22 line columns).
//
// REV 8 (18-Nov, Vijay: "why are the challan tables still there?"): the invoice is independent (rev 7), so the challan stubs have
//   no use in T24 and are REMOVED (CLAUDE.md: a stub is the smallest table the CURRENT task needs). Gone: sales.delivery_challan,
//   sales.delivery_challan_line (the sales schema), their 10 refs; fin.sales_invoice.delivery_challan_id, entry_mode,
//   challan_sent_back (+ 3 CHECKs, 1 index); fin.sales_invoice_line.delivery_challan_line_id, rate_from_order, batch_no,
//   batch_expiry_date (+ 2 CHECKs, 1 index); fin.e_way_bill.delivery_challan_id. The Stores task G30 designs its own challan and,
//   if an invoice must ever name a challan, adds that link then. T12 untouched.
//
// REV 9 (18-Nov, Vijay: "why 20+ columns?"): 5 columns nothing fills and nothing reads are removed: fin.sales_invoice
//   customer_po_no, customer_po_date (no PO on the CA's invoices; a buyer's order number goes in order_ref; G35 asks the CA),
//   e_commerce_gstin (the marketplace sale is G32); fin.e_way_bill transporter_name, transport_doc_no (the CA's invoices print the
//   mode and the vehicle only). Every remaining column has a source (typed, worked out, copied at Issue, or the approval trail)
//   and a reader; the walkthrough lists each with B4-01's value.
//
// REV 7 (18-Nov, T24 rev 5, Vijay): the sales invoice is INDEPENDENT, typed as the CA's 43 specimen invoices show it
//   (docs/sales/shared_doc_reference/Specimen_Sales_Invoices_All_43.pdf): no delivery challan, no sales order, no customer PO
//   on any of them (rev 9 removed the PO and e-commerce columns, rev 8 the challan ones).
//   +3 columns on fin.sales_invoice, homes for fields the CA's invoices print and the AE now types (release check 1):
//     transport_vehicle_no, transport_distance_km ("Transport: Road · KA-02-AB-1234"; the e-way bill's distance)
//     payment_reference ("Payment: UPI · Ref …", a sale paid at once, the CA's A3)
//   +2 CHECKs: sales_invoice_transport_distance_positive, sales_invoice_paid_now_has_reference. No table, key or ref changed.
//   Ledger choice is manual for now (Vijay, 18-Nov): the engine suggests, the AE may pick another ledger from a searchable list.
//   sales_invoice_entry_line.origin gains 'picked' (another ledger, the rule's amount): its reason is optional; 'changed' (an amount)
//   and 'added' still need one. The AM still sees every difference from the rule.
//   Every ledger is a suggestion (Vijay, 18-Nov), the customer and GST lines too: they may be 'picked' (their AMOUNT stays
//   the rule's = the invoice's). A picked customer line keeps debtor_account_id and carries the picked receivable ledger in
//   ledger_account_code. TO AGREE with Manoj (G36): his journal's commit check ACCOUNT_NOT_ON_ITS_LEDGER posts a customer
//   line only to its account's own ledger_account_code.
//
// REV 6 (16-Nov, T24 rev 3): the CA's 43 specimen sales invoices and their expected postings
//   (docs/sales/shared_doc_reference/expected_postings.json) read against T24. Two faults of ours corrected:
//   F1 an export / SEZ supply WITH IGST: the buyer owes the value only; the IGST is ours, claimed back (Dr IGST Refund Receivable).
//      New: igst_refund_amount, payable_amount (+ CHECKs). The customer line posts payable_amount.
//   F2 goods delivered to a THIRD PARTY on the buyer's direction: place of supply = the buyer's (bill-to) state (IGST s.10(1)(b)).
//      New: ship_to_third_party, place_of_supply_basis (+ CHECKs).
//   Added (the CA's cases): document_type (tax invoice · bill of supply · export invoice, each its own series); line taxability
//   (nil-rated, exempt, non-GST) and line kinds charge (freight, A8) and reimbursement (pure agent, B5); B2C (B2CS / B2CL) and
//   settlement (cash, UPI / card: no debtor needed for a B2C sale paid at once, A3); service period (deferred revenue, B6);
//   incoterm, the buyer's order reference, the advance adjusted (D7-b, posted by T13). Rule cases R8–R12.
//   e-invoicing (an IRN) applies to B2B, export and SEZ only: a Bill of Supply or a B2C sale posts without one.
// For dbdiagram.io: paste this whole file into the left editor. The T24 tables are at the end (REV 5 ADD-ON).
//
// REV 5 (16-Nov, Vijay): T24 Sales voucher entry added, built the way Manoj built the purchase voucher (engine repo
//   aikyat-business-engine-poc, branch manoj/vendor-backend-model, commit 923409b, migrations 0004–0006). 40 tables in all.
//   SYNC with what Manoj BUILT since our rev 4 (the customer side T24 reads; each change is his decision, followed):
//   S1 13-Nov "no control ledger" (docs/vendor-backend/ledger_and_subledger.md, migration 0005): fin.ledger_account loses
//      is_control and gains account_type (payable | receivable | stock | input_* | tds_payable; T24 adds output_*, tcs_payable,
//      round_off), since, until; fin.debtor_account.control_ledger_code is renamed ledger_account_code.
//   S2 identity keys his foreign keys use: fin.debtor_account (workspace_id, debtor_account_id), as built; T24 adds two on
//      fin.debtor_location (his table has none: hand-over item 3).
//   S3 clocks recorded_at / updated_at on fin.debtor_account, as built.
//   Not changed here (still "to agree", T12's open gaps): approvals in three parts vs the Accounts Manager alone (G9),
//   fin.outbox (G14), posting_blocked / sales_blocked and gst_state_code.legacy, a GSTIN with no address, names (G13).
//
// REV 4 (6-Nov, Vijay): our own faults found against Manoj's merged vendor + customer ER, corrected here:
//   F1 shipping address was typed but never stored → full address on the request; at approval a pc.business_location
//      "Ship-to" + fin.debtor_location (usage ship_to). fin.debtor_billing_location is renamed fin.debtor_location (usage bill_to | ship_to).
//   F2 four *_contact_id columns had no link to pc.contact → every *_contact_id is now a foreign key (workspace_id, contact_id).
//   F3 the app's Submit checks were not written in the schema → listed on fin.customer_request (CHECKS AT SUBMIT).
//   F4 B1 rows: the status and the default were not said → status from the GST portal check; the default is billing_gstin.
//   F5 one contact only (the vendor side has a list) → contacts jsonb list, the CA form's contact is the first entry.
//   NEW customer bank account (Vijay, 6-Nov; NOT on the CA form; optional) → Manoj's fin.party_bank_account, shared:
//      one row per party, so a customer who is also our vendor keeps one row. debtor_account.refund_bank_account_id.
//
// SOURCES (who wins, in order)
//   Fields   the CA's Customer Master onboarding form (Swasthik, 1. Customer_Master_Onboarding_Form_1.xls: A–F, B1)
//            as built in the app (app/sales, src/onboarding/rules.ts), plus two inputs not on the form:
//            shipping city / state / PIN (the law: IGST Act s.10(1)(a) place of supply; CGST Rule 46 delivery address;
//            the e-way bill's ship-to PIN) and the optional bank details (Vijay, 6-Nov)
//   Model    Manoj's vendor model (Vendor Creation Walkthrough_manoj_schema.html): same entities, names and flow,
//            customer side. VendorRequest → CustomerRequest · CreditorAccount → DebtorAccount · Party (P&C)
//   Ledgers  the CA's chart of accounts (Swasthik, 1. Global_Chart_of_Accounts_Updated_1.xls): debtor ledgers
//            107010–107130 (Indian GAAP) / 110010–110130 (Ind AS); customers numbered separately (C000001 …)
//   Party    Business Core, Parties and Contacts (schema pc, migrations 002–007): followed, never changed
//
// THE FLOW (Manoj's, one transaction)
//   Teja (AE) types the paper form → submits → three checkers approve their part (the app's routing):
//   Rajan (account), Farid (credit limit), Venkat (GST / TCS). On the LAST approval, ONE transaction:
//   P&C RegisterCustomer (party, identifiers, locations, standing, contact) + OpenDebtorAccount + finish the request.
//   If anything fails, nothing is saved and the request stays submitted. Nothing posts to the books.
//
// NAMING RULES (AI-friendly: a name says what the row is, without reading the code)
//   1. Tables are singular business nouns, owner-prefixed by schema: fin.debtor_account, pc.party.
//      Customer-side words mirror the vendor side exactly: vendor_request ↔ customer_request, creditor_* ↔ debtor_*.
//   2. A primary key is <table>_id (request_id, history_id inside history tables). A pointer keeps the target's name:
//      party_id, debtor_account_id. Its role is a prefix: opened_from_request_id, approved_by_contact_id.
//   3. Reference data is keyed by a readable *_code (debtor_category_code, account_code), never a number.
//   4. Booleans read as a yes/no question: gst_registered, export_customer, posting_blocked, is_default, is_primary.
//   5. Amounts end in _amount, dates in _from / _until / _date, instants in _at; people are *_contact_id: always a
//      foreign key (workspace_id, *_contact_id) → pc.contact (workspace_id, contact_id), and the contact is a person.
//   6. Every row has workspace_id (row-level security); every change writes a *_history row with row_snapshot.
//   7. One fact, one place: a value worked out from another (tax type, header) is not stored; the category holds
//      the CA's ledger, GST and TCS words; the account copies only what it must keep if the category later changes.
//
// Lines: black = foreign key inside one owner · grey = a foreign key into P&C (same database), declared with workspace_id
// as Manoj's merged ER does: (workspace_id, x_id) → pc.<table> (workspace_id, x_id). (rev 3 said "a plain value": changed in rev 4.)
// Example ids in notes (OP-1, P-1, DA-1 …) are the walkthrough's labels; real ids are uuids.

// =====================================================================
// pc · Parties and Contacts (Business Core's tables, NOT ours: the 11 this task reads or writes)
// =====================================================================

Table pc.operated_party [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  operated_party_id uuid [pk, note: 'OP-1: our company, the one this workspace runs']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null, note: 'P-OUR = Aikyat Technologies']
  since date
  until date
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, operated_party_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'P&C says which party is our company. THE company key in Finance (as on the vendor side): no legal_entity_id, no separate book key.'
}

Table pc.private_anchor [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  anchor_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  anchor_kind text [not null, note: 'organization | person']
  created_at timestamptz [not null]
  Note: 'A hidden identity row behind every party and contact; its id becomes party_id / contact_id. Finance never reads it.'
}

Table pc.party [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  party_id uuid [pk, note: 'same value as its private_anchor']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_kind text [not null, note: 'person | organization']
  legal_name text
  legal_form_code text [note: 'pc.legal_form: IN_PVT_LTD … (only IN_PVT_LTD is seeded today)']
  recorded_at timestamptz
  updated_at timestamptz
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, party_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'Who the customer is, as a legal person.'
}

Table pc.party_trade_name [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  trade_name_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null]
  name text [not null, note: 'the form\'s Customer Name']
  since date
  until date
}

Table pc.party_identifier [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  party_identifier_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null]
  scheme_code text [not null, note: 'IN_PAN | IN_GST']
  jurisdiction_scope_code text [not null, note: 'IN for PAN; the GST state for a GSTIN, e.g. IN-TN']
  value text
  standing_code text [note: 'GST: active | suspended | cancelled | none_held']
  since date
  until date
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, party_identifier_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'PAN and EVERY GSTIN (main + B1) of the customer. Not unique by design: a match never merges by itself. No location column.'
}

Table pc.business_location [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  business_location_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null]
  display_name text [not null, note: 'Registered office · Chennai branch · Ship-to']
  address_lines "text[]"
  locality text [note: 'city']
  administrative_area_code text [note: 'IN-KA']
  postal_code text
  country_code text
  since date
  until date [note: 'a closed place gets an until; never deleted']
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, business_location_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'A physical place of the customer: the registered address, each B1 branch address, a shipping address. Carries no GSTIN.'
}

Table pc.party_standing [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  party_standing_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null]
  operated_party_id uuid [not null]
  standing_type_code text [not null, note: 'customer (to be seeded in P&C: no standing types are seeded yet)']
  trade_name_id uuid
  since date
  until date
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, party_standing_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'P-1 is a customer OF our company OP-1. A party already our vendor gets a second standing, never a second party.'
}

Table pc.contact [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  contact_id uuid [pk, note: 'same value as its private_anchor']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  contact_kind text [not null, note: 'person | system: every Finance *_contact_id must be a person (checked by the action; a foreign key cannot)']
  display_name text [not null]
  accountable_party_id uuid
  destination_role text
  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, contact_id) [unique, note: 'the P&C key: the target of every Finance foreign key']
  }
  Note: 'The customer\'s contact person, and our own staff (Teja, Rajan, Farid, Venkat, Arun): every *_contact_id in Finance points here.'
}

Table pc.contact_party_association [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  association_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  contact_id uuid [not null]
  party_id uuid [not null]
  capacity_code text [not null, note: 'customer_contact (to be seeded)']
  since date
  until date
}

Table pc.contact_point [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  contact_point_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  contact_id uuid [not null]
  channel_kind text [not null, note: 'phone | email']
  route_value text
  business_location_id uuid
  since date
  until date
}

Table pc.record_history [headercolor: #0F766E] {
  history_id uuid [pk]
  workspace_id text [not null]
  record_type text [not null]
  record_id text [not null]
  version_number integer [not null]
  operation text [not null]
  row_snapshot jsonb [not null]
  performer_contact_id uuid
  recorded_at timestamptz
  Note: 'P&C\'s own history: one row per P&C record written.'
}

Ref: pc.operated_party.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.party.party_id > pc.private_anchor.anchor_id  // rev 5: was "-" (one-to-one), which exported the key on private_anchor → party AND contact: no row could satisfy both. P&C: party_id IS its anchor_id
Ref: pc.contact.contact_id > pc.private_anchor.anchor_id  // rev 5: as above
Ref: pc.party_trade_name.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.party_identifier.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.business_location.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.party_standing.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.party_standing.operated_party_id > pc.operated_party.operated_party_id
Ref: pc.party_standing.trade_name_id > pc.party_trade_name.trade_name_id
Ref: pc.contact_party_association.contact_id > pc.contact.contact_id
Ref: pc.contact_party_association.party_id > pc.party.party_id
Ref: pc.contact_point.contact_id > pc.contact.contact_id
Ref: pc.contact_point.business_location_id > pc.business_location.business_location_id
Ref: pc.record_history.performer_contact_id > pc.contact.contact_id
Ref: pc.contact.accountable_party_id > pc.party.party_id

// =====================================================================
// fin · Finance: reference data (set up before this task; read only here)
// =====================================================================

Table fin.ledger_account [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  account_code text [pk, note: 'the CA\'s 6-digit code: [group 1][sub-group 2][ledger 3], e.g. 107010']
  display_name text [not null, note: 'Trade Receivables – Domestic']
  kind text [not null, note: 'asset | liability | equity | income | expense']
  parent_account_code text [note: 'the group above it']
  is_group boolean [not null, default: false]
  account_type text [note: 'rev 5 (Manoj 13-Nov, built; replaces is_control): payable | receivable (each line names a vendor / customer) | stock | input_cgst | input_sgst | input_igst | tds_payable | T24: output_cgst | output_sgst | output_igst | tcs_payable | round_off. NULL on a group']
  since date
  until date
  checks {
    `account_type IN ('payable', 'receivable', 'stock', 'input_cgst', 'input_sgst', 'input_igst', 'tds_payable', 'output_cgst', 'output_sgst', 'output_igst', 'tcs_payable', 'round_off')` [name: 'ledger_account_account_type_valid']
    `NOT (is_group AND account_type IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ledger_account_group_has_no_type']
    `kind IN ('asset', 'liability', 'equity', 'income', 'expense')` [name: 'ledger_account_kind_valid']
    `parent_account_code <> account_code` [name: 'ledger_account_not_its_own_parent']
  }
  Note: '''The chart of accounts: groups and ledgers in one table. SHARED with the vendor side (Manoj\'s table, same columns).
Rev 5: no "control ledger" (Manoj, 13-Nov): a ledger whose account_type is receivable takes only lines that name a customer account.
Seed (task S2, Chart of accounts): the CA\'s chart. Debtor control ledgers 107010–107130 (Indian GAAP) or 110010–110130 (Ind AS):
the framework decides the code (gap G4). Employee advances: 105030 (not a trade receivable).'''
}

Table fin.debtor_category [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  debtor_category_code text [pk, note: 'DOMESTIC_B2B_REGULAR …']
  display_name text [not null, note: 'Domestic B2B Trade Debtor – Regular']
  ledger_account_code text [not null, note: 'the control ledger the category names: nobody picks a ledger on the form']
  customer_type_code text [note: 'b2b | b2c | b2g: what the category implies (a warning if the form differs)']
  international boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'an export category: Export customer must be Yes']
  related_party boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Ind AS 24 disclosure + elimination']
  gst_treatment_note text [note: 'the CA\'s words, e.g. Zero-rated (with LUT/Bond, or with IGST + refund)']
  tcs_section_code text [note: '394(1) | 394 | SEC52_CGST | NULL = not applicable']
  tcs_rate_note text
  credit_period_note text [note: 'the CA\'s words: 30 Days · As per contract / Advance']
  default_credit_days integer [note: 'only when the CA gives one number (30, 45)']
  notes text
  sort_order integer [not null]
  Note: '''The CA\'s Debtor Category Master: 16 rows, the twin of fin.creditor_category (20 rows).
It drives the form\'s "System suggestions". The invoice task reads gst_treatment / tcs from here (as the vendor bill reads creditor_category).
Seed, category → ledger (Indian GAAP · Ind AS):
DOMESTIC_B2B_REGULAR 107010 · 110010 | DOMESTIC_B2C_RETAIL 107020 · 110020 | DOMESTIC_B2G 107030 · 110030 | EXPORT_GOODS 107040 · 110040
EXPORT_SERVICES 107040 · 110040 | SEZ_UNIT 107050 · 110050 | DEEMED_EXPORT 107060 · 110060 | ECOMMERCE_MARKETPLACE 107070 · 110070
RELATED_PARTY_DOMESTIC 107080 · 110080 | RELATED_PARTY_OVERSEAS 107090 · 110090 | DEALER_DISTRIBUTOR 107100 · 110100
SCRAP_SPECIFIED_GOODS 107110 · 110110 | LUXURY_GOODS 107120 · 110120 | TOUR_PACKAGE 107130 · 110130
COMPOSITION_SCHEME 107010 · 110010 (Trade Receivables – Domestic) | EMPLOYEE_ADVANCES 105030 (Employee Advances Receivable)'''
}

Table fin.gst_state_code [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  gst_code text [pk, note: '29']
  iso_3166_2 text [note: 'IN-KA; NULL for 96 Other Countries, 97 Other Territory, 99 Centre Jurisdiction (rev 5: nullable, as built)']
  display_name text [not null]
  legacy boolean [not null, default: false]
  Note: 'Turns the first two digits of a GSTIN into a state. SHARED with the vendor side (41 rows). Our GST home state (Karnataka, 29) is a company setting.'
}

Ref: fin.ledger_account.parent_account_code > fin.ledger_account.account_code
Ref: fin.debtor_category.ledger_account_code > fin.ledger_account.account_code

// =====================================================================
// fin · CustomerRequest: what Teja typed, and who approved which part
// =====================================================================

Table fin.customer_request [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  request_id uuid [pk, note: 'R-101']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  operated_party_id uuid [not null, note: 'our company OP-1']
  request_kind text [not null, default: 'create']
  stage text [not null, default: 'draft', note: 'draft | submitted | active | returned | rejected']
  version_number integer [not null, default: 1]
  paper_form_number text [not null, note: 'CRF-0021: the paper Sales handed over']
  handed_over_by_contact_id uuid [not null, note: 'Arun (Sales Executive)']

  // A. Basic details
  customer_name text [note: 'Customer Name → pc.party_trade_name']
  legal_name text [note: '→ pc.party']
  constitution_code text [note: 'individual | proprietorship | partnership | llp | company | huf | trust_society | government | foreign_entity | other → party_kind + legal_form_code']
  customer_type_code text [note: 'the form\'s Customer Category: b2b | b2c | b2g']
  debtor_category_code text [note: 'drives the suggestions; gives the ledger']
  pan text [note: '→ pc.party_identifier IN_PAN']
  resident_status text [note: 'resident | non_resident']
  account_name text [note: 'NULL = customer_name; unique in the books']

  // B. GST & tax compliance (+ B1)
  gst_registered boolean
  gstin text [note: 'the main GSTIN → pc.party_identifier IN_GST']
  gst_registration_type text [note: 'regular | composition | sez | government | unregistered | other']
  gst_registration_count integer [note: 'how many in additional_gst_registrations']
  additional_gst_registrations jsonb [not null, default: '[]', note: '''B1, up to 15: [{gstin, registration_type, address_lines, locality, postal_code, remarks}].
The state is not typed: it comes from the GSTIN's first two digits (fin.gst_state_code). The status (active | suspended | cancelled)
is not typed either: the GST portal check gives it, and it lands in pc.party_identifier.standing_code (a cancelled GSTIN stops Submit).
Which GSTIN is the default is billing_gstin, not a flag in the list. At approval each entry → pc.party_identifier + fin.debtor_tax_registration,
and, when it has an address, pc.business_location + fin.debtor_location (bill_to).''']
  billing_gstin text [note: 'Default / Billing GSTIN; NULL = the main one → debtor_tax_registration.is_default']
  export_customer boolean
  sez_customer boolean
  e_invoice_applicable text [note: 'yes | no | not_applicable']
  tcs_applicability_confirmed text [note: 'yes | no | not_applicable']

  // C. Address & contact
  address_lines "text[]" [note: '→ pc.business_location (Registered office)']
  locality text
  administrative_area_code text [note: 'IN-KA']
  postal_code text
  country_code text
  shipping_address_lines "text[]" [note: 'the CA form\'s Shipping Address (if different) → pc.business_location "Ship-to" + fin.debtor_location (ship_to)']
  shipping_locality text [note: 'rev 4, not on the CA form: needed with the lines']
  shipping_administrative_area_code text [note: 'rev 4: the ship-to state decides the place of supply for goods (IGST Act s.10(1)(a))']
  shipping_postal_code text [note: 'rev 4: the e-way bill needs the ship-to PIN']
  shipping_country_code text [note: 'rev 4; default IN']
  contacts jsonb [not null, default: '[]', note: '''rev 4, as vendor_request: [{name, phones[], emails[]}]. Entry 1 is the CA form\'s Contact Person,
Mobile Number and Email ID; more can be added. At approval each entry → pc.contact + pc.contact_party_association (customer_contact),
and each phone / email → pc.contact_point. A blank name: the customer name is used (placeholder rule, to confirm with P&C).''']

  // Bank details: optional, NOT on the CA form (Vijay, 6-Nov). Same column names as vendor_request.
  bank_beneficiary text [note: 'account holder name → fin.party_bank_account']
  bank_name text
  bank_branch text
  bank_account_no text
  bank_ifsc text
  bank_proof_attached boolean [note: 'cancelled cheque / bank letter']

  // D. Credit & collection terms, and the billing instructions (task card T12)
  credit_or_cash text [note: 'credit | cash']
  credit_limit_amount "numeric(18,2)" [note: '→ fin.debtor_credit_limit']
  payment_terms_code text [note: 'immediate | net_15 | net_30 | net_45 | net_60 | net_90 | other']
  credit_days integer
  security_deposit_amount "numeric(18,2)"
  collection_owner_contact_id uuid
  currency_code text [default: 'INR']
  bill_wise_tracking boolean [default: true]
  po_reference_rule text [note: 'every_invoice | none']
  invoice_delivery_channel text [note: 'email | portal | with_goods | courier']
  invoice_format_note text

  // E. Export / cross-border receipts (only if export_customer)
  ad_bank_name text
  export_category_code text [note: 'goods | services | goods_services']
  lut_number text
  lut_valid_until date
  realisation_mode_code text [note: 'advance | brc_firc | lc | other']
  firc_brc_tracking boolean
  edpms_softex_reference text

  // F. Documents & remarks
  gst_certificate_attached boolean
  pan_copy_attached boolean
  msme_certificate_attached boolean
  remarks text

  // Lifecycle: written only by the actions
  created_by_contact_id uuid [not null, note: 'Teja']
  submitted_by_contact_id uuid
  submitted_at timestamptz
  submission_number integer [not null, default: 0, note: '+1 at each submit; approvals belong to one submission']
  accepted_warnings "text[]" [note: 'warnings Teja accepted at submit']
  return_reason text [note: 'stage = returned: the paper went back to Sales']
  party_id uuid [note: 'set when Teja uses a party P&C already holds, else at activation']
  debtor_account_id uuid [note: 'set at activation: a plain pointer (a second foreign key would make a circle)']
  activated_at timestamptz
  recorded_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  Indexes {
    (workspace_id, operated_party_id, stage) [name: 'customer_request_queue_ix', note: 'Needs me · With checkers · Done']
  }
  Note: '''One customer form, as typed: the twin of fin.vendor_request. It keeps what was typed even after approval.
Typed fields are nullable: a draft may be half filled; the checks run at Submit (the form\'s mandatory rules).
CHECKS AT SUBMIT (rev 4: written here; the app runs them, src/onboarding/rules.ts). STOP = cannot submit · WARN = accept with a reason.
  Fill   every mandatory field of the CA form (red), each conditional one when its condition holds (amber)            STOP
  PAN    AAAAA0000A · GSTIN 15 characters, its PAN part = the PAN, a known state code · same for every B1 GSTIN        STOP
  GST    gst_registered = false ⇒ gstin, gst_registration_type, gst_registration_count NULL and B1 empty                 STOP
  B1     at most 15 · no GSTIN listed twice · billing GSTIN needed when more than one · the billing GSTIN has an address   STOP
  Portal each GSTIN checked on the GST portal: cancelled STOP · suspended WARN · name differs WARN (gap G10: deferred)
  State  the main GSTIN's state = the registered address state                                                         STOP
  Place  PIN 6 digits (registered, B1, ship-to) · a shipping address needs its city, state and PIN                      STOP
  People mobile 10 digits · email valid · at least one mobile and one email · each extra contact has a name             STOP
  Export export category ⇔ export_customer · section E only when export_customer · no LUT → IGST paid route             STOP / WARN
  Match  customer category vs the category's · SEZ / composition type vs the category · TCS not confirmed               WARN
  Credit credit ⇒ credit_limit_amount and credit_days set · amounts >= 0 · credit_days vs payment terms (WARN)        STOP / WARN
  Bank   all or nothing: account no, IFSC and beneficiary together · IFSC ^[A-Z]{4}0[A-Z0-9]{6}$ (STOP) ·
         the account held by another party (WARN, BANK_ACCOUNT_IN_USE) · the same party already holds it: reused, no new row
  Once   the PAN / GSTIN already has an open customer account in this company: STOP (ALREADY_OUR_CUSTOMER); a known party: reuse it
  Name   account_name unique in the books                                                                              STOP
  Maker  decider <> submitted_by_contact_id                                                                            STOP
Not stored (worked out each time): tax type (GSTIN state vs our home state), the header above the ledger, the
GST / TCS words (from the category). CHECKs: stage list; returned ⇒ return_reason; active ⇒ party_id and debtor_account_id.'''
}

Table fin.customer_request_approval [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  approval_id uuid [pk, note: 'AP-1 …']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  request_id uuid [not null]
  submission_number integer [not null]
  approval_part_code text [not null, note: 'account | credit_limit | tax']
  approver_role_code text [not null, note: 'accounts_manager | credit_controller | compliance_officer']
  approver_contact_id uuid [note: 'who decided (Rajan · Farid · Venkat)']
  outcome text [not null, default: 'pending', note: 'pending | approved | sent_back | rejected']
  decided_at timestamptz
  reason text [note: 'needed for sent_back and rejected']
  approved_values jsonb [note: 'the fields of this part as approved: an unchanged part stays approved on resubmit']

  Indexes {
    (request_id, submission_number, approval_part_code) [unique]
  }
  Note: '''The app routes one submit to three checkers, each their part (task card T12: the Credit Controller checks the limit).
Vendor side has one decision column set (decided_by, decision); the customer has three parts, so a child table.
Rule: approver_contact_id <> customer_request.submitted_by_contact_id. The LAST approval runs the one transaction.'''
}

Table fin.customer_request_history [headercolor: #4F46E5] {
  history_id uuid [pk, note: 'RH-1 …']
  workspace_id text [not null]
  request_id uuid [not null]
  version_number integer [not null]
  operation text [not null, note: 'create | save | submit | send_back | return_form | reject | activate']
  performer_contact_id uuid
  reason text
  row_snapshot jsonb [not null, note: 'the full request row at this version']
  recorded_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  Note: 'One row per version of the request (as fin.vendor_request_history).'
}

